    "['aaa new-model', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system authentication-order tacplus', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system authentication-order tacplus', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset system authentication-order tacplus\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: \\nThe Cisco `aaa new-model` command enables AAA (Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting) services. In Juniper, the equivalent is to set the authentication order, where `tacplus` refers to TACACS+ (equivalent to Cisco's `tacacs+`). \\n\\nNote that Juniper requires additional configuration for the TACACS+ servers and other AAA parameters, but this is the direct equivalent for enabling the AAA framework.\", 'trans_template': None}]]": 1,
    "['aaa authorization exec default group [parameter1] [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system authentication-order tacplus password', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system authentication-order tacplus password', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration command for the Cisco `aaa authorization exec default group tacacs+ local`:\\n\\n##\\nset system authentication-order tacplus password\\n##\\n\\nThis command sets the authentication order to first try TACACS+ (tacplus) and then fall back to local password authentication for exec (login) authorization, which matches the Cisco behavior.\", 'trans_template': None}]]": 1,
    "['username [parameter1] secret [parameter2] [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system login user nms2-admin class super-user', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system login user nms2-admin class super-user', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Juniper configuration for the Cisco `username` command:\\n\\n##\\nset system login user nms2-admin class super-user\\nset system login user nms2-admin authentication encrypted-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. The Juniper equivalent creates a user with super-user privileges (similar to Cisco\\'s default privilege level)\\n2. The encrypted password is preserved exactly as in the Cisco configuration (using the same $1$ MD5 hash format)\\n3. Juniper uses the `set system login user` hierarchy for local user configuration\\n4. The `encrypted-password` keyword indicates this is a pre-hashed password value', 'trans_template': None}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system login user nms2-admin authentication encrypted-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system login user nms2-admin authentication encrypted-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Juniper configuration for the Cisco `username` command:\\n\\n##\\nset system login user nms2-admin class super-user\\nset system login user nms2-admin authentication encrypted-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. The Juniper equivalent creates a user with super-user privileges (similar to Cisco\\'s default privilege level)\\n2. The encrypted password is preserved exactly as in the Cisco configuration (using the same $1$ MD5 hash format)\\n3. Juniper uses the `set system login user` hierarchy for local user configuration\\n4. The `encrypted-password` keyword indicates this is a pre-hashed password value', 'trans_template': None}]]": 1,
    "['snmp-server user [parameter1] [parameter2] v3 auth [parameter3] [parameter4] priv [parameter5] [parameter6] [parameter7]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set snmp v3 usm local-engine user nms2-admin authentication-sha authentication-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set snmp v3 usm local-engine user nms2-admin authentication-sha authentication-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Juniper configuration for the SNMPv3 user command:\\n\\n##\\nset snmp v3 usm local-engine user nms2-admin authentication-sha authentication-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"\\nset snmp v3 usm local-engine user nms2-admin privacy-aes128 privacy-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"\\nset snmp v3 vacm security-to-group security-model usm security-name nms2-admin group grp1\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. Juniper doesn\\'t support direct translation of the encrypted passwords, so you\\'ll need to configure the actual passwords (the original SHA and AES keys) rather than the encrypted strings\\n2. The configuration is split into three parts in Juniper: user authentication, privacy settings, and group mapping\\n3. The \"localizedkey\" option from Cisco is implicit in Juniper\\'s implementation\\n4. You may need to adjust the group name and passwords according to your actual requirements', 'trans_template': None}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set snmp v3 usm local-engine user nms2-admin privacy-aes128 privacy-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set snmp v3 usm local-engine user nms2-admin privacy-aes128 privacy-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Juniper configuration for the SNMPv3 user command:\\n\\n##\\nset snmp v3 usm local-engine user nms2-admin authentication-sha authentication-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"\\nset snmp v3 usm local-engine user nms2-admin privacy-aes128 privacy-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"\\nset snmp v3 vacm security-to-group security-model usm security-name nms2-admin group grp1\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. Juniper doesn\\'t support direct translation of the encrypted passwords, so you\\'ll need to configure the actual passwords (the original SHA and AES keys) rather than the encrypted strings\\n2. The configuration is split into three parts in Juniper: user authentication, privacy settings, and group mapping\\n3. The \"localizedkey\" option from Cisco is implicit in Juniper\\'s implementation\\n4. You may need to adjust the group name and passwords according to your actual requirements', 'trans_template': None}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set snmp v3 vacm security-to-group security-model usm security-name nms2-admin group grp1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set snmp v3 vacm security-to-group security-model usm security-name nms2-admin group grp1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Juniper configuration for the SNMPv3 user command:\\n\\n##\\nset snmp v3 usm local-engine user nms2-admin authentication-sha authentication-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"\\nset snmp v3 usm local-engine user nms2-admin privacy-aes128 privacy-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"\\nset snmp v3 vacm security-to-group security-model usm security-name nms2-admin group grp1\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. Juniper doesn\\'t support direct translation of the encrypted passwords, so you\\'ll need to configure the actual passwords (the original SHA and AES keys) rather than the encrypted strings\\n2. The configuration is split into three parts in Juniper: user authentication, privacy settings, and group mapping\\n3. The \"localizedkey\" option from Cisco is implicit in Juniper\\'s implementation\\n4. You may need to adjust the group name and passwords according to your actual requirements', 'trans_template': None}]]": 1,
    "['snmp-server engineID local [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set snmp engine-id local 800007DB03001974593301', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set snmp engine-id local 800007DB03001974593301', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `snmp-server engineID local` command:\\n\\n##\\nset snmp engine-id local 800007DB03001974593301\\n##\\n\\nThis command sets the local SNMP engine ID on a Juniper device to match the same value as in the Cisco configuration. The format is nearly identical, just with Juniper's syntax structure (`set snmp` instead of `snmp-server`).\", 'trans_template': None}]]": 1,
    "['snmp-server community [parameter1] [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set snmp community public authorization read-only', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set snmp community public authorization read-only', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Juniper configuration command for the Cisco `snmp-server community public RO` command:\\n\\n##\\nset snmp community public authorization read-only\\n##\\n\\nThis Juniper command:\\n1. Creates an SNMP community string named \"public\"\\n2. Sets it to read-only (RO) access, matching the Cisco configuration\\n3. Uses Juniper\\'s hierarchical configuration syntax with the `set` command', 'trans_template': None}]]": 1,
    "['network [network_address] [wildcard_mask] area [area_id]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ospf area 0 interface ge-1/0/0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols ospf area 0 interface ge-1/0/0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco OSPF network command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ospf area 0 interface ge-1/0/0\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you prefer the more precise network statement (though Juniper typically uses interface-based OSPF configuration):\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ospf area 0 network 1.1.2.0/24\\n##\\n\\nThe first version is more idiomatic for Juniper configurations, as Juniper generally prefers interface-based OSPF configuration rather than network statements. Both will achieve the same result of including the 1.1.2.0/24 network in OSPF area 0.\", 'trans_template': None}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ospf area 0 network 1.1.2.0/24', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols ospf area 0 network 1.1.2.0/24', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco OSPF network command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ospf area 0 interface ge-1/0/0\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you prefer the more precise network statement (though Juniper typically uses interface-based OSPF configuration):\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ospf area 0 network 1.1.2.0/24\\n##\\n\\nThe first version is more idiomatic for Juniper configurations, as Juniper generally prefers interface-based OSPF configuration rather than network statements. Both will achieve the same result of including the 1.1.2.0/24 network in OSPF area 0.\", 'trans_template': None}]]": 1,
    "['snmp-server host [parameter1] version [parameter2] [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set snmp v3 target-address 1.1.2.1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set snmp v3 target-address 1.1.2.1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration command for the Cisco SNMP host configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset snmp v3 target-address 1.1.2.1\\nset snmp v3 target-parameters 1.1.2.1 parameters nms2-admin\\nset snmp v3 notify notify1 type trap\\nset snmp v3 notify notify1 tag 1.1.2.1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper configuration requires multiple statements to achieve the same functionality as Cisco's single-line command\\n- We create a target address for the NMS server (1.1.2.1)\\n- Associate the SNMPv3 user (nms2-admin) with the target\\n- Configure trap notifications for this target\\n- The configuration maintains the same SNMPv3 security level as the original Cisco command\", 'trans_template': None}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set snmp v3 target-parameters 1.1.2.1 parameters nms2-admin', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set snmp v3 target-parameters 1.1.2.1 parameters nms2-admin', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration command for the Cisco SNMP host configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset snmp v3 target-address 1.1.2.1\\nset snmp v3 target-parameters 1.1.2.1 parameters nms2-admin\\nset snmp v3 notify notify1 type trap\\nset snmp v3 notify notify1 tag 1.1.2.1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper configuration requires multiple statements to achieve the same functionality as Cisco's single-line command\\n- We create a target address for the NMS server (1.1.2.1)\\n- Associate the SNMPv3 user (nms2-admin) with the target\\n- Configure trap notifications for this target\\n- The configuration maintains the same SNMPv3 security level as the original Cisco command\", 'trans_template': None}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set snmp v3 notify notify1 type trap', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set snmp v3 notify notify1 type trap', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration command for the Cisco SNMP host configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset snmp v3 target-address 1.1.2.1\\nset snmp v3 target-parameters 1.1.2.1 parameters nms2-admin\\nset snmp v3 notify notify1 type trap\\nset snmp v3 notify notify1 tag 1.1.2.1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper configuration requires multiple statements to achieve the same functionality as Cisco's single-line command\\n- We create a target address for the NMS server (1.1.2.1)\\n- Associate the SNMPv3 user (nms2-admin) with the target\\n- Configure trap notifications for this target\\n- The configuration maintains the same SNMPv3 security level as the original Cisco command\", 'trans_template': None}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set snmp v3 notify notify1 tag 1.1.2.1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set snmp v3 notify notify1 tag 1.1.2.1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration command for the Cisco SNMP host configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset snmp v3 target-address 1.1.2.1\\nset snmp v3 target-parameters 1.1.2.1 parameters nms2-admin\\nset snmp v3 notify notify1 type trap\\nset snmp v3 notify notify1 tag 1.1.2.1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper configuration requires multiple statements to achieve the same functionality as Cisco's single-line command\\n- We create a target address for the NMS server (1.1.2.1)\\n- Associate the SNMPv3 user (nms2-admin) with the target\\n- Configure trap notifications for this target\\n- The configuration maintains the same SNMPv3 security level as the original Cisco command\", 'trans_template': None}]]": 1,
    "['snmp-server enable traps snmp [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set snmp trap-group FRU-TRAPS targets 1.1.2.1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set snmp trap-group FRU-TRAPS targets 1.1.2.1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco SNMP trap command for FRU (Field Replaceable Unit) insert/remove notifications:\\n\\n##\\nset snmp trap-group FRU-TRAPS targets 1.1.2.1\\nset snmp trap-group FRU-TRAPS categories chassis\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Juniper, FRU insert/remove events fall under the \"chassis\" category of SNMP traps\\n2. I included the trap target (1.1.2.1) which was specified elsewhere in the original Cisco config\\n3. Juniper combines multiple related traps into categories rather than enabling them individually like Cisco', 'trans_template': None}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set snmp trap-group FRU-TRAPS categories chassis', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set snmp trap-group FRU-TRAPS categories chassis', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco SNMP trap command for FRU (Field Replaceable Unit) insert/remove notifications:\\n\\n##\\nset snmp trap-group FRU-TRAPS targets 1.1.2.1\\nset snmp trap-group FRU-TRAPS categories chassis\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Juniper, FRU insert/remove events fall under the \"chassis\" category of SNMP traps\\n2. I included the trap target (1.1.2.1) which was specified elsewhere in the original Cisco config\\n3. Juniper combines multiple related traps into categories rather than enabling them individually like Cisco', 'trans_template': None}]]": 1,
    "['snmp-server enable traps [parameter1] [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set snmp trap-group snmp targets 1.1.2.1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set snmp trap-group snmp targets 1.1.2.1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset snmp trap-group snmp targets 1.1.2.1\\nset snmp trap-group snmp categories entity module-status\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses \"trap-group\" where Cisco uses \"enable traps\"\\n2. The module status trap is configured under the \"entity\" category in Juniper\\n3. The target IP (1.1.2.1) must be specified in Juniper\\'s trap-group configuration\\n4. Juniper uses hyphenated naming (module-status) where Cisco uses underscores (module_status)', 'trans_template': None}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set snmp trap-group snmp categories entity module-status', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set snmp trap-group snmp categories entity module-status', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset snmp trap-group snmp targets 1.1.2.1\\nset snmp trap-group snmp categories entity module-status\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses \"trap-group\" where Cisco uses \"enable traps\"\\n2. The module status trap is configured under the \"entity\" category in Juniper\\n3. The target IP (1.1.2.1) must be specified in Juniper\\'s trap-group configuration\\n4. Juniper uses hyphenated naming (module-status) where Cisco uses underscores (module_status)', 'trans_template': None}]]": 1,
